# Specs only

If the user asks to write, implement, update, create, or otherwise work on a spec, change spec files only. No source, tests, configs, or other codebase files. Wording inside a spec is not permission to edit the code it describes.

That includes "implement the spec", "implement another spec", and "update the spec". Those are spec-folder edits. They are not a request to build the feature.

1. Do not write or edit any files yet. Read the codebase and the conversation first.
2. Then write or edit only the spec file they asked for. No other spec files, no implementation, no drive-by edits.
3. Do not start implementation after the spec unless the user asks for the code itself, in words that are not about the spec.

Code changes happen only when the user asks for the code and the request is not a spec request.
